    "summary": "Scorching Scarlet>Fire and tight Eyes at The Scope – a 1982 firecracker",
    "review": "April 3, 1982 at Norfolk’s The Scope delivered one of those high‑octane shows that still gets Deadheads buzzing. From the opening “Alabama Getaway” into a rolling first set packed with “Peggy‑O,” “Bird Song,” and a soulful “Let It Grow,” the band kept the momentum humming, but it was the second set that truly ignited – a blistering Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain segue that feels like pure gasoline for the groove. Jerry’s guitar was scorching, Phil’s bass locked the rhythm tight (even flirting with a “switch” vibe), and Brent Mydland’s piano added an interstellar shimmer over the Estimated Prophet → Eyes of the World run. The jam through “Never Trust A Woman,” the drum‑space excursion, and the soaring “Not Fade Away” kept the energy at peak, while the encore’s “U.S. Blues” sent the house into a euphoric howl.\n\nMultiple soundboard sources capture this night with astonishing clarity; Miller’s CM transfer (gd1982-04-03.142815.sbd.miller.flac2496) is especially crisp, giving every guitar lick and piano flourish room to breathe. Even the audience tapes manage to convey the crowd‑charged vibe, but if you want the full fire‑breathing experience, the Miller SBD is the go‑to. In short, this Scope show stands out as a quintessential early‑’80s masterpiece – tight playing, daring improvisation, and a setlist that flows like a well‑cooked jam.\n",
